column147:
09/Jun/19 3:05 PM;557058:fa089759-8cd1-4db3-b506-5731a37fd5a6;If there’s a single user with a single pseud where the username and email both match the query, I don’t think it’ll double-count the user. But if there’s a user with multiple pseuds (matching or not), and the user’s email matches the query, it will include each of the user’s pseuds in the count.

The reason this happens is because this join causes MySQL to create one row for each of the pseuds of the user with the matching email:

{code}users.joins(:pseuds).where("pseuds.name = ? OR email = ?", query.to_s, query.to_s){code}

The duplicate rows don’t show up in the results because of this line:

{code}users = User.select("DISTINCT users.*").order(:login){code}

But because the query has {{DISTINCT}} in the call to {{select}}, instead of using the {{distinct}} scope, {{paginate}} doesn’t know that it’s only supposed to count distinct items, so it doesn’t remove duplicates when computing the count. That results in the discrepancy. It should be possible to fix it by replacing that line of code with:

{code}users = User.distinct.order(:login){code}

column27:
When performing a POST to any API end-point, a 404 response is returned. Looking in the logs, this appears to be due to the following error:

{code}
method=POST path=/api/v1/bookmarks format=json controller=Api::V1::BookmarksController action=create status=422 error='ActionController::InvalidAuthenticityToken: ActionController::InvalidAuthenticityToken' duration=1.12 view=0.00 db=0.00
{code}

This is due to a change in the way Rails handles cross-domain requests and no longer prepends {{protect_from_forgery}} to the {{before_action}} chain. However, we don't want to do cross-domain verification for the API anyway as it will always be requested by an external server, so the solution will be to disable it in the API::BaseController using {{skip_before_action :verify_authenticity_token}}

