column148:
05/Sep/18 6:57 PM;557058:f99670ac-6f0b-469b-bdfb-a9817a67932f;From [What Is Relevance?|https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/guide/2.x/relevance-intro.html]
{quote}
How long is the field? The longer it is, the less likely it is that words in the field will be relevant. A term appearing in a short title field carries more weight than the same term appearing in a long content field.
{quote}

You can see that happening in the "Harry" work search: at the top are works with very short "harry" titles, then works with author names matching exactly "harry", then works with "harry" in longer titles, etc.

column27:
On Travis, we check if Elasticsearch is up using:
{code:bash}
wget -q --waitretry=1 --retry-connrefused -T 20 -O - http://127.0.0.1:9400
{code}

wget can handle connection refused errors, but there's small window where ES is not fully up but can already respond with a 503 instead of a 200, which fails wget.

We should use the [cluster health API|https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/6.2/cluster-health.html] to wait:
{code:bash}
wget -q --waitretry=1 --retry-connrefused -T 20 -O - "http://127.0.0.1:9400/_cluster/health?wait_for_status=yellow"
{code}

See also the article "[Dealing with 503 errors when testing Elasticsearch integration in Rails|https://medium.com/@thetron/dealing-with-503-errors-when-testing-elasticsearch-integration-in-rails-ec7a5f828274]".

How to test: Travis builds should pass consistently.

On Codeship, we should use the [cluster health API|https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/6.2/cluster-health.html] to wait for Elasticsearch to fully start:
{code:bash}
wget -q --waitretry=1 --retry-connrefused -T 20 -O - "http://127.0.0.1:9400/_cluster/health?wait_for_status=yellow"
{code}

How to test: none, automated. This is the Codeship version of AO3-5491 (for Travis).

