column27:
If you put the following {{blockquote}} of some Hebrew text (stolen from [the translation of Naomi's speech at the Hugos|https://archiveofourown.org/admin_posts/13531]) in a work, it will produce a block of text with a border on the left side, which is weird in right to left languages:



{code:html}<blockquote dir="rtl">
  פרס ההוגו הזה יצטרף לנדידתה של התערוכה המגיעה לכל Worldcon, כיוון שהוא שייך לכולנו. אני רוצה לבקש להרים את האורות, ושמי מכם שמרגיש חלק מהקהילה שלנו שיעמוד לרגע ויחלוק אותו איתנו.
</blockquote>{code}



Likewise, a {{blockquote}} included in an FAQ that's written in a RTL language will have the border in the same odd place (these {{blockquote}} elements generally won't have {{dir="rtl"}} on them directly – they get the direction from a parent {{div}}).

Ergo, we need to add some CSS to basically flip the styling we use in LTR languages:



{code:css}.userstuff[dir="rtl"] blockquote:not([dir="ltr"]),
.userstuff blockquote[dir="rtl"] {
  margin: auto 1.5em auto auto;
  border-left: none;
  border-right: 2px solid #999;
}{code}



The first selector should take care of {{blockquote}}s in the FAQ, while the second should take care of {{blockquote}}s in works (and in news posts until [https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-5735#icft=AO3-5735|https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-5735#icft=AO3-5735|smart-link] is done).



*Testing notes*

Also try including a {{blockquote}} with the direction set to ltr in a Hebrew or Arabic FAQ and make sure the border is still on the left.



{code:html}<blockquote dir="ltr">
  This is in English. The border should be on the left.
</blockquote>{code}

